A||N||13||Ozzy||Ign. Coil & Alternator cooling fan??||||5||
Z||000000||Ozzy||04-12-2000||01:07 AM||||Hi Gang...<P>I found the rear blower system on my '87 GT. (Now I know what those wierd looking silver pipes are!!!) It is not working at present. I check the fan motor by powering it direct and it's fine. I jumpered the relay connector pins and also the blower runs...oddly, if I jumper the other 2 pins the coolant fan runs.<P>I'm getting 12v at the relay base too but it is hard to determine what the exact problem is. I pulled the relay apart to check it out. If I apply 12v directly to the coil the relay works...But I can find no sense in the continuity of the relay base pins to the coil or otherwise. I'm assuming that the relay might have a diode/capacitor in the base of the relay which has failed.(??)<P>Has anyone replaced this relay or had a similar problem? If so, where did you get the relay? (The one listed as "single stage cooling fan relay for '85-'87" on the Fiero Store site does not look quite the same.<P>TIA<P>Ozzy||||reg||5
Z||000001||WKDFIRO||04-12-2000||04:26 AM||||With the engine running turn on your AC. The radiator fan should go on at the same time as the coolant fans. The wire is a green with white stripe. It goes on ONLY when it is grounded. I have never heard of this fan failing but this is a way to test in a normal situation. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||TheFiero64||04-12-2000||10:59 AM||||The trunk fan is only suppose to run when the coolant fan is on. If your rad fan is off then the trunk one will be too. That's why the fan runs when you jumped it. Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ozzy||04-12-2000||04:02 PM||||O.K....Thnx guys.<P>Is the relay near the rear fan the coolant fan relay or is that in the front compartment. <P>Right now, the air cond. is not working. Also when I turned on the Air to check to see if the coolant fan fired up, (which it did not) I smelled burning wire insulation! No smoke though so I'm o.k. for the moment but need to get a handle on this wiring system and relays for the fans.<P>Ozzy<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||04-12-2000||04:16 PM||||Ain't Fieros fun?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ozzy||04-12-2000||11:39 PM||||UPDATE!<P>I've been a messin' with the blue beast...Here's what I found. <P>After buying a new relay from NAPA today along with a fan switch and coolant sensor, I started doing some checking. As sugested above I shorted the fan switch wire to ground and the blower and coolant fan both fired up. (With both the old & the new relay!! Good thing it was only $8.00!) So I swapped out the fan switch and cycled the engine. Whala! Both kicked in at about 230 on the temp guage. Thats a little hotter than I want to run it but at least it works.<P>Next, I started looking into the Air Cond. system. It is short on freon determined by shorting the press. cycle switch. But the coolant fun does not run when the air is running. I looked thru the relevant wiring schematics in the Haynes manual and as far as I can see the coolant fan is not supposed to run automatically when the air is on with the V6. It is with the 4 cyl and does on my Coupe. Can any one add some clarity to this?<P>Also, I read one of ogres posts stating that you need to run a different eeprom if you run the 175 deg. fan switch  and 165 deg. thermostat. Anyone???<P>Thnx<P>Ozzy||||reg||4
Z||000006||Ed||04-13-2000||12:23 AM||||The front radiator fan does normally come on when the A/C is engaged in the V-6.  <P>I sometimes turn on the A/C in summer commute traffic to cool off the engine because of the fans (at least the front, for sure) coming on.<P>P.S. I went and got the big shop manual for the 87's.  Both the coolant fan and engine blower are supposed to run with A/C on.  Man, what a trouble shooting tree if one works and the other doesn't, etc.!   <p>[This message has been edited by Ed (edited 04-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||WKDFIRO||04-13-2000||03:19 AM||||I too, don't like seeing the temp gauge going anywhere near 220. And sometimes I don't want to loose power to the AC if Im sharking around for parking on a normal day. A manual fan switch is my friend! I spliced off from the green wire w/ white stripe coming from behind the AC unit and ran the wire (I used an identical green w/white wire from a bad wiring harness.) to an old switch that the original owner put in to power his under car lights. (They looked real cool in the trash can where I put them.) The switch toggles the wire to a ground bolt which turns on the fan and the cooling tubes. Very easy. Also give me piece of mind. With the fan on I can stay at 195 all day long in traffic without buring up the extra fuel for AC.||||reg||3
Z||000008||Phil||04-13-2000||08:07 AM||||J.C. Whitney sells an adjustable temp fan switch for about $15||||reg||1
Z||000009||Ozzy||04-13-2000||10:20 AM||||Thnx Guys...<P>The adjustable switch sounds cool! Ed...Neither fans come on when the Air is turned on. This may be a relay or there are perhaps other pressure switches my crappy Haynes manual does not show. Blower and AIR power relays are both fine. Still working on it...<P><BR>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000010||Phil||04-13-2000||12:48 PM||||The air conditioning control only grounds the existing relay. My 85 was found to have a burnt out trace in the printed circuit in the AC control head most likely do to the creative rewiring done by the previous owner.  In reality it was kinda fun chasing that one down. Sat down with my Haynes volt/ohm meter some jumper wires and a BIG cup of coffee. I used the diagrams on pgs. 281 and 283 bottom pics.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Phil (edited 04-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||04-13-2000||01:51 PM||||The V6 and distributor 4cyl ECM doesn't care what fan switch you put in. Also doesn't care if it's a 180 or 195 thermostat. A 160-165 thermostat can be a problem tho. Those are really ment for use with a specific PROM in the ECM. ((Most aftermarket PROMS do not require and may not work right with the 165 thermostat. The one that does is Hypertec I think.))||||reg||1
Z||000012||lennyb4||04-13-2000||02:08 PM||||DDuuuhhh...OK fella's now im completly lost..Do all fiero's have a rear fan or only models w/air? I have a 87 Coupe 4 banger (for now..hehe) w/o air. And if it doesnt, would adding a small electric fan help any with cooling or serve any other purpose?||||reg||5
Z||000013||Standard||04-13-2000||02:51 PM||||Only the 85-87 V6 cars had a trunk mounted blower, to try to keep the temp of the ign. coil and alternator down.  Doesn't really work to well since the v6 still has a tendancy to eat coils.||||reg||1
